//? Title: Depthwise Separable Convolution: Cost and Rank
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A dense spatial convolution mixes every input channel with every output channel at every
tap. Factoring spatial filtering from channel mixing reduces cost while restricting the
filters it can represent. We compose both factors into a dense tensor kernel and check
forward values, gradients and the multiplier-dependent rank bound.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.1_fundamentals/depthwise_separable.cpp`.

> The method

For depthwise D[a,b,i,m] and pointwise P[iM+m,o], the equivalent dense filter is

$$
W[a,b,i,o]=\sum_{m=0}^{M-1}D[a,b,i,m]P[iM+m,o],\qquad
\operatorname{rank}(W_i)\le M.
$$
\alttext(Each input channel's spatial-by-output filter is a product through M intermediate channels, so its rank is at most M.)

A k×k dense layer has k² Cin Cout weights; depthwise plus pointwise has
k² Cin M+Cin M Cout. The ratio is M/Cout+M/k², with the same multiply-count
ratio per output site. This is a count, not a measured latency guarantee.
The composition holds only without intervening nonlinearities or an intermediate bias.
With M=1 each input channel shares a single spatial pattern across output channels;
increasing M can recover higher ranks. Tensor matmul composes factors differentiably.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/depthwise_separable_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/depthwise_separable_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/depthwise_separable_concept.tikz > build/depthwise_separable_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/depthwise_separable_concept.svg build/depthwise_separable_concept.dvi > build/depthwise_separable_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
