> Checking the theory

A rank-two spatial/output target cannot be represented with multiplier one. Its second singular value gives the best possible rank-one residual in Frobenius norm.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto type = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto target = n::tensor({1, 0, 0, 0, 2, 0, 0, 0, 0}, {3, 3}, type);
  const auto singular = n::svd(target).s;
  const double rank_one_error = n::sum(n::square(n::slice_axis(singular, 0, 1, 3))).item();
  if (std::abs(rank_one_error - 1) > 1e-12)
    return 1;
  std::print("Target singular values {}; minimum rank-one squared residual {}.\n",
             singular.to_vector<double>(), rank_one_error);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Target singular values [2, 1, 0]; minimum rank-one squared residual 1.
// result_end


> Conclusion

The composed kernel and both factor gradients match the two-stage operator. The multiplier
controls both cost and a precise expressivity limit; inserting a ReLU changes the function
class and removes this single-linear-kernel equivalence.

> References

- [Howard et al. (2017), MobileNets](https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.04861).
- [Dumoulin and Visin (2016), A guide to convolution arithmetic for deep learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.07285).
